The given circuit is a <strong>Sallen--Key second-order active low-pass filter</strong>. The non-inverting gain of the op-amp is \[A_{v}=1+\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{17\,\text{k}\Omega}
=1.588\] Hence, the low-frequency gain in dB is \[20\log_{10}(1.588)\approx 4\,\text{dB}.\] The cutoff frequency is \[f_{c}=\frac{1}{2\pi RC}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(22\,\text{k}\Omega)(330\,\text{pF})}
\approx 21.9\,\text{kHz}.\] Since it is a <strong>second-order</strong> low-pass filter, the attenuation beyond the cutoff frequency is \[-40\,\text{dB/decade}.\] For the Butterworth response, the gain at the cutoff frequency is \(3\,\text{dB}\) below the passband gain: \[4\,\text{dB}-3\,\text{dB}=1\,\text{dB}.\] Therefore, the correct frequency response has: <ul><li>Passband gain \(=4\,\text{dB}\),</li><li>Gain at \(f_{c}=21.9\,\text{kHz}\) equal to \(1\,\text{dB}\),</li><li>Roll-off of \(40\,\text{dB/decade}\).</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{(c) Passband gain }=4\,\text{dB},\ f_{c}=21.9\,\text{kHz},\ \text{roll-off}=40\,\text{dB/decade}}\]

The left transistor is diode-connected, hence its base and collector are shorted. The reference current is \[I_{R}=\frac{2-0.7}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}=1.3\,\text{mA}\] Since all three transistors are identical, they have the same \(V_{BE}\), so their base currents are equal. Applying KCL at the reference node, \[I_{R}=I_{C1}+I_{B1}+I_{B2}+I_{B3}\] Using \(I_{C}=\beta I_{B}\), \[1.3\,\text{mA}=(\beta+3)I_{B}\] For \(\beta=100\), \[I_{B}=\frac{1.3\,\text{mA}}{103}=12.62\,\mu\text{A}\] Hence, \[I_{C}=100\times12.62\,\mu\text{A}=1.262\,\text{mA}\] The two right transistors draw \[I=2I_{C}=2.524\,\text{mA}\] Therefore, \[V_{C}=10-(2.524\,\text{mA})(1\,\text{k}\Omega)
      =7.476\,\text{V}
      \approx7.4\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }7.4\,\text{V}}\]

The open-loop transfer function with a proportional controller \(K\) is: \[G(s)H(s) = \dfrac{6K}{(s+1)(s+2)(s+3)}\] The characteristic equation of the closed-loop system is \(1 + G(s)H(s) = 0\): \[(s+1)(s+2)(s+3) + 6K = 0 \implies s^3 + 6s^2 + 11s + (6 + 6K) = 0\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Using the Routh-Hurwitz array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 11 \\
s^2 &amp; 6 &amp; 6(1+K) \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{66 - 6(1+K)}{6} &amp; 0 \\
s^0 &amp; 6(1+K) &amp; 
\end{array}\] For marginal stability, the \(s^1\) row becomes zero, giving the auxiliary equation from the \(s^2\) row: \[6s^2 + 6(1+K) = 0\] Substituting \(s = j\omega = j\sqrt{11}\) into the auxiliary equation: \[6(j\sqrt{11})^2 + 6(1+K) = 0\] \[-66 + 6 + 6K = 0 \implies K = 10\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div><strong>Shortcut Method (Inner-Outer Product):</strong><br/>For a standard third-order characteristic equation \(a_3s^3 + a_2s^2 + a_1s + a_0 = 0\): \[s^3 + 6s^2 + 11s + 6(1+K) = 0\] At the verge of instability (marginal stability), the product of the inner coefficients equals the product of the outer coefficients: \[\text{Inner Product} = \text{Outer Product}\] \[a_2 \cdot a_1 = a_3 \cdot a_0\] \[6 \times 11 = 1 \times 6(1+K)\] \[66 = 6 + 6K\] \[K = 10\]</div> \[\boxed{\text{(C) 10}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><strong>Inner-Outer Product Rule:</strong> For a stable third-order system \(s^3 + as^2 + bs + c = 0\), marginal stability occurs when \(ab = c\), and the frequency of sustained oscillations is \(\omega = \sqrt{b}\).</div>

The transfer function of the inner loop is \[G_i(s)=\frac{K_2}{2s^2+3s+1+K_2}\] The outer loop forward path is, \[G_{eq}(s)=\frac{K_1}{3s+1}\,G_{i}(s)=\frac{K_1}{3s+1} \times \frac{K_2}{2s^2+3s+1+K_2}=\frac{K_1K_2}
